

Maintenance Automation – Direct SSH Implementation

1. Overview
This document describes the implementation of Maintenance Automation using Direct SSH (JSch) instead of TagUI.
The objective is to improve:


Performance


Reliability


Scalability


Maintainability


by removing unnecessary UI automation dependencies.

2. Background (Existing TagUI-Based Approach)
Previously, TagUI was used to execute CLI commands on Juniper devices.
Existing Flow


Commands were generated dynamically


Commands were converted to TagUI scripts


Working directories were created


Credential files were decrypted


.tag files were generated


TagUI process was executed


Output was saved in text files


Output was parsed


Existing Architecture
Spring Boot → TagUI → SSH → Juniper Device

Limitations


Heavy dependency


Slow execution


No true parallel support


Difficult debugging


Limited error handling



3. Proposed Solution: Direct SSH (JSch)
The new implementation removes TagUI and uses direct SSH communication.
New Architecture
Spring Boot → JSch → Juniper Device

New Execution Flow


Open SSH session


Authenticate


Execute CLI commands


Capture output


Handle errors


Close session


All operations are managed inside the application.

4. Benefits of Direct SSH
|| Area || TagUI || Direct SSH ||
| Performance | Slow | Fast |
| Dependency | Heavy | Light |
| Control | Limited | Full |
| Debugging | Difficult | Easy |
| Scalability | Low | High |
| Security | File-based | In-memory |
Key Improvements


Faster execution


Better reliability


Better monitoring


Easier retry handling


Improved security



5. Impact on Maintenance Automation Flow
Pre-check / Config / Post-check
No functional change.
Only execution method is updated.
Old: Spring → TagUI → SSH → Device  
New: Spring → SSH → Device

Uplink Redundancy Test
The redundancy logic remains unchanged:
Disable Interface → Ping → Enable Interface

Only the execution layer is replaced.

6. Parallel Execution Support
Direct SSH enables:


Multiple concurrent SSH sessions


Thread pool execution


Independent connections


This allows multiple maintenance flows to run in parallel.

7. Lock and Retry Integration
The Direct SSH implementation integrates with:


Device-level locking


Global redundancy locking


Automatic retry mechanism


This ensures safe and reliable execution.

